\title{Smart Contract Design, Specification, and Verification}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-08-29}

\p{
    % [Smart contracts](dhsorens-000Y) are programs that execute on the blockchain.
    [Smart contracts](dhsorens-000Y) are interesting from the perspective of formal specification and verification because:
}
\ol{
    \li{they are risk- and property-dense,}
    \li{they are typically small in size, with a deterministic and well-defined execution environment, making them feasible to formally reason about in a short time frame, and}
    \li{they often manage huge sums of money (so the incentives line up).}
}
\p{
    Furthermore, because smart contracts act as social and financial intermediaries, they often have extremely interesting (and [nontrivial](dhsorens-000L)) specifications, which warrants a study not only of the science of going from specification to verification (formal proof), but of the \em{specifications themselves} as independent, mathematical objects.
}